525
00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:05.000
Mifflin, but in true Michael fashion, he waits until the very last day of October, which also

526
00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:06.000
happens to be Halloween.

527
00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:07.880
Greg Daniels wrote this episode.

528
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:12.280
And I remember him telling me that the inspiration for the episode was that he had always wanted

529
00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:16.860
to do a story about someone being fired, but from the point of view of the boss rather

530
00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:17.580
than the employee.

531
00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:22.440
He said, we always see stories of the people being fired, but rarely stories about the person

532
00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:23.500
having to do the firing.

533
00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:27.000
And I always thought that Greg's decision to set the story on Halloween when everyone

534
00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:31.640
is dressed up and excited for our office Halloween party made the whole thing even more delightfully

535
00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:32.720
uncomfortable to watch.

536
00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:33.700
Oh, so tragic.

537
00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.600
Like no one wants to get fired when they're dressed up like a clown or something.

538
00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:38.300
It's depressing.

539
00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:43.540
But the one problem that this plot presented for Greg was that he would actually have to

540
00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:45.180
fire someone on the show.

541
00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:49.520
And he didn't want to fire any of the core cast members, even though many of the supporting

542
00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:52.260
characters at this time were still under weekly contracts.

543
00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:55.300
You guys were not considered series regulars yet.

544
00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:56.300
Oh no, not at all.

545
00:20:56.300 --> 00:21:00.600
You know how you say you have a photo of the exact moment you knew we'd be best friends?

546
00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:01.000
Yes.

547
00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.720
I have a photo of the exact moment I was made series regular.

548
00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:09.560
And you and I are jumping up and down, giddy, holding hands, jumping up and down because

549
00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:11.680
I got the news while we were on Booze Cruise.

550
00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:13.700
That's when we became series regulars.

551
00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:15.840
But this was before Booze Cruise.

552
00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:16.420
Yes.

553
00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:20.420
So he could have any of us fired any of you guys, but he didn't want to.

554
00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:26.740
But he also didn't want to hire a new person just for one episode, establish them, and fire

555
00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:28.280
them because it felt dishoneSt.

556
00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:30.320
Like it felt, he said it felt like cheating.

557
00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:33.240
So he had to fire someone we'd already seen on the show.

558
00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:35.440
So we had two stand-ins at the time.

559
00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:37.720
And maybe we should tell people what a stand-in is.

560
00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:42.040
A stand-in is a person who fills in for the actor after the rehearsal of a scene while

561
00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:43.180
the crew adjusts the lights.

562
00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:46.760
They walk where the actor would walk or sit or stand where the actor would sit or stand

563
00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:47.340
during the scene.

564
00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:49.420
And the crew figures out where to put the cameras and the lights.

565
00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:53.260
And then when it's time for the camera to roll, the actors are called back to the set

566
00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:54.620
and begin shooting the scene.

567
00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:56.080
So we had two stand-ins.

568
00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:57.680
They were Creed and Devin.

569
00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:02.740
And both Creed and Devin had also appeared on camera in the background of some conference

570
00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:05.560
room scenes or in the annex by the break room.

571
00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:09.220
Maybe they would like cross in the deep background of a scene like they were delivering mail.

572
00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:13.440
I remember we had that mail cart that really made no sense because we didn't have a mail

573
00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:14.700
room, but whatever.

574
00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:16.040
But they'd never spoken.

575
00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:17.000
They'd been established.

576
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:18.100
So that was enough for Greg.

577
00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:25.980
But then Greg, who is the gentlest man, had the dilemma of picking who to fire, Creed or

578
00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:26.540
Devin.

579
00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:32.740
I can't imagine how that weighed on Greg because he really is just a kind, kind-hearted person.

580
00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:35.740
And it just must have wrecked him.

581
00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:37.880
So Greg spoke with both of them.

582
00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:44.180
And he found out that Devin had just been cast in an off-Broadway play in New York.

583
00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:46.640
So this was all Greg needed to hear.

584
00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:50.100
He decided Devin would be fired since he had this job to go to.

585
00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:50.580
He had something to go to.

586
00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:57.160
And Creed went from being a non-speaking stand-in in our show to getting a five-page scene with

587
00:22:57.160 --> 00:22:57.800
Steve Carell.

588
00:22:58.000 --> 00:22:58.780
No audition.

589
00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:00.700
Greg had never seen him act.

590
00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:02.400
Handed him a five-page scene with Steve Carell.

591
00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:08.320
The scene was so popular with fans and Greg was so delighted that he started writing more

592
00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:09.000
stuff for Creed.

593
00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:11.900
And then Creed eventually also became a series regular.

594
00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:14.360
And it was all because Greg needed someone to fire.

595
00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:21.500
So in addition to this being the episode that launched Creed into the Office family, it is

596
00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:23.480
responsible for a number of firsts.

597
00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:26.940
This is the episode that features Jim and Pam's first air high five.

598
00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:30.180
Aw, jam, jam, air high five.

599
00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:31.340
Jam, air high five.

600
00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:37.660
It is also the first time Pam is seen wearing her hair down rather than half up, half down,

601
00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:39.100
which I call the Pam mullet.

602
00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:43.260
And it was a huge debate about whether or not this hair change could be allowed.

603
00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:46.540
It went several rounds with the producers and writers before being approved.

604
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:50.200
Would Pam look too cute if she wore her hair down?

605
00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:51.600
They decided to go for it.

606
00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:56.040
And then the next time you see a hair change for Pam is casino night.

607
00:23:56.240 --> 00:24:00.500
I mean, if we know a lot of details about hair and stuff like that is because these were

608
00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:01.840
huge discussions.

609
00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:06.900
We just always wanted to stay true to the integrity of who these people were and not

610
00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:10.960
as the success of the show grew, like, zhuzh them up too much.

611
00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:16.380
They were still in Scranton in a paper company, even though the show was gaining more popularity

612
00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:21.680
and we were sort of becoming a little bit like, I don't know, like fancier.

613
00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:24.500
Well, this was true of wardrobe as well.

614
00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:29.620
They only shopped at places that our characters could realistically afford to shop at.

615
00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:34.140
So if Pam had to wear a pair of jeans, I wore a pair of jeans from the Gap.

616
00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:37.000
I did not wear a pair of $200 designer jeans.

617
00:24:37.300 --> 00:24:37.460
Right.

618
00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:39.700
And also our wardrobe department was amazing.

619
00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:42.700
And, you know, they made sure like your pants weren't too long or things like that,

620
00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:46.940
but they did not fit you, like alter things the way most TV shows do.

621
00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:50.340
And after the office, I worked on a show called Your Family or Mine.

622
00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:53.820
And the women that did the wardrobe also did the wardrobe for Friends.

623
00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:56.120
And those women looked amazing on Friends.

624
00:24:56.300 --> 00:25:01.060
And I have never looked so kabam like ever in my life.

625
00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:03.320
They altered it, everything.

626
00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:07.700
They altered like where your shoulder hem is and where like the ends,

627
00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:09.980
like everything fit me like a glove.

628
00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:10.420
Yes.

629
00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:13.240
Most shows tailor clothes to your body.

630
00:25:13.400 --> 00:25:13.540
Yeah.

631
00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:15.840
And that's why everybody looks so fabulous on TV.

632
00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:19.680
They said to me, do you know what's the difference between just a normal person walking down the street

633
00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:21.360
and someone walking down the street on a TV show?

634
00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:22.340
And I said, what?

635
00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:23.560
And they said alterations.

636
00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:25.520
It's 100% true.

637
00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:29.120
Because most people like figure out how to blow out their hair and put a little bit of makeup on.

638
00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:30.920
But having clothes fit you like that.

639
00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:35.320
And they were blown away because I was like, oh my gosh, look how this fits me.

640
00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:36.580
Oh, you're going to take that in?

641
00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:37.320
You're going to sew this?

642
00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:40.140
And they're like, Angela, have you never had your clothes altered on the office?

643
00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:41.860
I was like, no.

644
00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:42.460
No.

645
00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:46.420
Because we had to look realistic for that world.

646
00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:55.440
Another thing about Halloween is people dress up as our office characters for Halloween.

647
00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:56.280
On Halloween.

648
00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:57.460
Which I love so much.

649
00:25:57.480 --> 00:25:58.480
Which is crazy, right?

650
00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:01.800
When I get tagged as white and Angela, I love it so much.

651
00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:02.760
You are a Halloween costume.

652
00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:03.120
Yeah.

653
00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:08.840
So I also want to say that of all the photos that I get, I think that the most popular,

654
00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.960
the one I see the most, is the Pam Cat three-hole punch gym.

655
00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:12.680
Mm-hmm.

656
00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:13.220
Yeah.

657
00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:14.220
Those are the ones.

658
00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:15.180
That's the one I get the moSt.

659
00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:20.360
I get just myself in an Argyle sweater with usually the girls have their hair in a headband

660
00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:24.300
or a slicked-back pony, and they're all holding a cat-stuffed animal.

661
00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:28.000
I get a lot where girls are wearing the white cats.

662
00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:33.480
Another firSt.

663
00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:38.020
This Halloween episode was the first time since our pilot that the creators of the

664
00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:42.120
original British series of The Office, Stephen Merchant and Ricky Gervais, visited our set.

665
00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:43.200
Yes.

666
00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:44.980
They stayed almost an entire day.

667
00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:47.520
One day when we were filming, we were all geeking out.

668
00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:52.280
Before they left, I asked for a photo with them and some of the American producers of the

669
00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:56.820
show, but I am the only person in the photo who is obviously dressed in a Halloween costume.

670
00:26:57.020 --> 00:26:57.560
Do you have it?

671
00:26:57.640 --> 00:26:57.820
Yes.

672
00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:01.760
You can kind of see, like, Jim has, like, one circle of his three-hole punch.

673
00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:02.120
Yes.

674
00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:05.940
When I look at the photo, I like to imagine that I was doing, like, a school play about

675
00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:09.500
a cat, and all these people came to see me, and I posed for a picture backstage.

676
00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:11.320
Yeah, that's backstage at your cat play.

677
00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:11.940
At my cat play.

678
00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:12.260
Yeah.

679
00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:15.520
Here's my one story about Ricky Gervais.

680
00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:17.180
So, they were on set.

681
00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:22.660
We were all kind of nervous about it, and I hadn't really been in scenes yet, but after

682
00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:29.100
lunch, I had a talking hedge, and I come through the lunch area, and I see Ricky Gervais, and

683
00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:33.000
I'm introduced to him, and they're like, Ricky's actually going back to Video Village now,

684
00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:36.280
because he's going to watch everything from lunch on to the end of the day.

685
00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:38.260
And you were the first thing you started watching?

686
00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:39.880
And I was like, great.

687
00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:41.500
And they were like, Angela, you're first up.

688
00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:43.000
And I said, oh, fantastic.

689
00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:47.700
And I looked to Ricky Gervais, and he goes, don't worry, I don't tsk too loudly.

690
00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:51.100
And that's what he said to me.

691
00:27:51.320 --> 00:27:52.220
That's amazing.

692
00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:52.680
I know.

693
00:27:53.000 --> 00:28:02.800
For all of these reasons, I love this first Halloween episode, but I especially love the

694
00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:03.060
end.

695
00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:06.880
And I don't know if you remember the end, but at the end, Michael hands out candy to

696
00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:08.480
little kids, and I cry every time.

697
00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:10.000
I'm getting choked up thinking about it.

698
00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:14.360
I remember there was a big debate over how that final moment should be played out.

699
00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:18.840
There was a discussion as to whether or not the kids should be nice to Michael, or if

700
00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:22.640
they should be annoyed by him, because he was going to be cracking jokes while he handed

701
00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:23.140
out candy.

702
00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:28.640
And the writers and producers also discussed if Steve should be happy or sad while handing

703
00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:29.160
out the candy.

704
00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:33.940
It was decided that they would start on a spy shot of Michael looking sad and lonely inside

705
00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:35.960
of his house, alone on Halloween night.

706
00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:37.780
He's just fired someone.

707
00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:42.240
Then you hear the doorbell, and when he gets to the door and sees the kids, he lights up,

708
00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:47.900
he makes his silly jokes, and the kids just laugh and laugh and are delighted by him, and

709
00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:48.700
he makes this connection.

710
00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:49.260
I'm crying.

711
00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:49.660
Yeah.

712
00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:52.720
And it's just beautiful and kind and heartbreaking.

713
00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:56.060
And it's moments like this that make The Office so special.

714
00:28:56.060 --> 00:29:00.280
We can spend an entire episode laughing at Michael Scott or finding him annoying, but in the

715
00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:04.960
end, he will break your heart with his kindness and vulnerability and his desire to have what

716
00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:09.160
we all want, which is just to love and be loved in return.

717
00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:09.980
Yes.

718
00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:15.480
And that is heartbreaking and so sweet and so real, isn't it?

719
00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:17.760
It's just like such an honest moment.

720
00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:24.060
And I think that's what I think Greg Daniels was so great at in our show is finding these

721
00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:26.740
just real life moments that were so honeSt.

722
00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:30.560
And you've got to know firing someone was so hard for Michael because all he wanted to

723
00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:31.420
do was be liked.

724
00:29:31.640 --> 00:29:35.340
And this dysfunctional office family was truly his family.

725
00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:41.680
It was such a great choice to have him go home and you see why he's so needy at work.

726
00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:42.320
Yes.

727
00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:42.760
You know?

728
00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:43.700
He's so lonely.

729
00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:44.780
He's just so lonely.

730
00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.360
And I loved that the kids delighted in him.

731
00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.360
And there's this thing that Steve did with his face.

732
00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:53.200
He just looked like lit up inside.

733
00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:53.840
You know?

734
00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:57.960
When they laughed, you saw that joy come over Michael.

735
00:29:58.320 --> 00:29:59.480
Oh, it was so sweet.

736
00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:06.260
If we ever get to do a reunion episode, I just need to see Michael Scott with his kid.

737
00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:06.980
Yes.

738
00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:08.600
And he's the best dad.

739
00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:09.340
The best dad.

740
00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:09.660
Yes.

741
00:30:09.800 --> 00:30:13.580
And his kid just thinks he is everything.

742
00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:13.880
Yeah.

743
00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:14.940
Exactly.

744
00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:16.580
And him and Holly are just happy.

745
00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:16.920
Yeah.

746
00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:17.960
It would make me so happy.

747
00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:18.260
I would cry.

748
00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:21.380
And maybe he still annoys people at work, but he's like the best dad.

749
00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:22.220
The best dad.

750
00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.880
And I think it hit me even harder now that I have kids.

751
00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:25.600
Yeah.

752
00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.300
Because I just know what kids bring to your life.

753
00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:36.930
The episode was written by resident genius Greg Daniels.

754
00:30:37.010 --> 00:30:38.710
It was directed by, do you know?

755
00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:39.590
Paul Feig.

756
00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:41.070
Oh, Paul Feig.

757
00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:45.150
Well, you know, I was going to guess Ken Coapis or Paul Feig because they're in the early

758
00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:45.390
years.

759
00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:46.170
That's a good guess.

760
00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:46.570
Yeah.

761
00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:50.490
And they also, we gave them a lot of the big, like milestone episodes.

762
00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:54.950
During his time on the show, I'm going to give you some fast facts about Paul Feig.

763
00:30:55.050 --> 00:31:00.590
He directed 14 episodes of The Office, but he also spent an entire season where he was

764
00:31:00.590 --> 00:31:01.590
a consulting producer.

765
00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:02.230
That's right.

766
00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:03.810
And he was there every day on set.

767
00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:06.090
When he left, I was like, no, Paul, don't.

768
00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:07.770
I felt so safe with him there.

769
00:31:07.870 --> 00:31:08.450
It was a dream.

770
00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:15.970
He also, I think, hosted the most cast viewing parties of anyone in his home with his incredible

771
00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:16.510
wife, Lori.

772
00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:17.090
Yes.

773
00:31:17.210 --> 00:31:18.310
And their home was beautiful.

774
00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:18.750
Beautiful.

775
00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:20.730
And just so warm and inviting.

776
00:31:20.930 --> 00:31:21.410
I loved it.

777
00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:26.890
They put out a little like snackity snack in the kitchen and then we would all get our

778
00:31:26.890 --> 00:31:28.770
stuff and we would go watch the show together.

779
00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:30.750
We did that so much.

780
00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:31.190
So much.

781
00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:31.590
And Paul.

782
00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:32.790
So many viewing parties.

783
00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:34.910
Paul and Lori are just such great party hosts.

784
00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:38.830
Paul is one of the best dressed people you will ever meet in your life.

785
00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:39.790
Full suit with tie.

786
00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.450
I've never not seen him in a suit.

787
00:31:41.590 --> 00:31:44.470
And his Instagram is all of him in suits with cocktails.

788
00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:47.010
You have to see it because it's very enjoyable.

789
00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:47.750
It's very impressive.

790
00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:49.770
Oh, we're about to deep dive on your feet on Instagram.

791
00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:50.610
Don't get freaked out.

792
00:31:51.890 --> 00:31:56.150
Also, did you talk about what the crew would do on Friday when it was Paul Feig's episode?

793
00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:57.590
They would all wear a tie.

794
00:31:58.130 --> 00:31:58.890
Oh, yes.

795
00:31:59.250 --> 00:31:59.470
Yeah.

796
00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:03.030
It would be Paul Feig Day, suit day, every Friday when Paul Feig directed.

797
00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:06.550
And all the crew members, the camera guys, they would all wear ties.

798
00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:12.700
Halloween did not end with our Halloween episodes.

799
00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:18.180
In order to edit the episodes in time to air during the actual week of Halloween, we filmed

800
00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:21.540
our Halloween episodes like usually in August, like months before.

801
00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:23.880
So every year we got two Halloween celebrations.

802
00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:27.800
First was the week that we shot the Halloween episode, but then there was actual Halloween.

803
00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:31.700
And during actual Halloween week, our crew would all dress up and we would have a costume

804
00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:32.460
parade at lunch.

805
00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:34.660
And the crew went all out.

806
00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:35.080
Yeah.

807
00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:38.560
We would shoot a whole day with them all in costumes and us dressed as our characters.

808
00:32:38.840 --> 00:32:43.080
One year, I don't know if you remember this, the wardrobe department dressed as the characters

809
00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:43.760
from our show.

810
00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:47.380
And I have pictures of them dressed as the characters.

811
00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:48.460
That is hilarious.

812
00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:49.980
Although this guy was a props guy.

813
00:32:50.120 --> 00:32:53.000
He wasn't wardrobe, but wardrobe dressed as us.

814
00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:54.600
Oh my gosh, that's amazing.

815
00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:55.360
I know.

816
00:32:55.460 --> 00:32:56.140
It was pretty great.

817
00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:03.900
We were like one big family.

818
00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:04.740
We were.

819
00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:05.580
Not just the caSt.

820
00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:06.240
It was the crew.

821
00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:06.720
Yes.

822
00:33:06.860 --> 00:33:07.900
We looked out for one another.

823
00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:09.060
We enjoyed being together.

824
00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.920
Work days were long, but they were not as long as a typical television show.

825
00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:18.860
No.

826
00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:22.800
We averaged about 12 hours a day and it usually was something like 6 a.m.

827
00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:23.460
to 6 p.m.

828
00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:26.940
It was rare to go as late as 7.30, but it did happen occasionally.

829
00:33:27.140 --> 00:33:27.240
Yeah.

830
00:33:27.380 --> 00:33:29.960
But it never happened on Halloween.

831
00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:31.760
We were always wrapped early.

832
00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:32.740
Always.

833
00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:36.900
And I think it's because so many of our crew members had families with young kids and

834
00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.060
the producers wanted to make sure that everyone was home in time to celebrate with their families.

835
00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:41.660
That's right.

836
00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:45.300
And you know, Greg himself is a very big family man.

837
00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:47.400
I think that those things are important.

838
00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:48.460
They were important to him.

839
00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:55.620
And I think when you're on a show where your showrunner creator has a family, they just go

840
00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:57.660
into it thinking about people's families.

841
00:33:57.820 --> 00:33:58.980
I always felt that way.

842
00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:05.540
I felt like they knew when I went to them when I had Isabel and I was going to be bringing

843
00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:06.200
her to set.

844
00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:09.620
I felt like I could go and have a conversation and they would help make it work.

845
00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:13.680
I know that it was very important to Steve that he had home for dinner with his family

846
00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:14.120
every night.

847
00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:14.520
Yes.

848
00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:17.780
He would come in first thing in the morning, 5.30, 6 a.m.

849
00:34:18.140 --> 00:34:20.540
So that he could be finished by 5.30 at night.

850
00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:25.580
And then some of the people who didn't have kids would stay later and then work to that

851
00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:29.200
6, 6.30 time frame happily, you know, for him.

852
00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:33.040
In fact, there were a lot of younger people who preferred coming in at 8 a.m.

853
00:34:33.200 --> 00:34:33.640
and working

854
00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:34.200
later.

855
00:34:34.200 --> 00:34:34.480
Right, right.

856
00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:35.900
They did not want to come in at 5.30.

857
00:34:36.220 --> 00:34:39.280
But once you have kids, you're awake anyway, you know.

858
00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:39.460
Yeah.

859
00:34:39.580 --> 00:34:41.300
So you don't want to miss bedtime.

860
00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:43.000
You don't want to miss that dinner and bedtime.

861
00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:47.980
And it can be made to work if the people who are in charge make it work.

862
00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:50.420
And it happened on Halloween.

863
00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:56.340
Also, every year, Matt Sohn, who was one of our camera operators, and then later he

864
00:34:56.340 --> 00:35:00.860
became our director of photography and even directed some episodes, he would throw a party

865
00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:03.220
at his house after work for everyone and their families.

866
00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:05.360
And he lived in this great neighborhood for trick-or-treating.

867
00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:09.940
And we'd show up and he would have these little tables that had crafts for the kids.

868
00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:10.600
Oh, he had.

869
00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:11.180
It was so cute.

870
00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.400
Their, like, front room of their house had little low tables with little chairs.

871
00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:15.040
Yes.

872
00:35:15.120 --> 00:35:16.820
It was like kid paradise over there.

873
00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:18.560
Little stickers, projects.

874
00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:19.900
You could make a little pumpkin.

875
00:35:20.220 --> 00:35:20.460
Uh-huh.

876
00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:21.600
And then food.

877
00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:22.760
They'd order pizza.

878
00:35:23.140 --> 00:35:24.980
And then we'd just go out and canvass the neighborhood.

879
00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:27.340
And even cast members without kids would come.

880
00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:30.000
I know BJ Novak used to go over and hand out candy.

881
00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:30.400
Yeah.

882
00:35:30.460 --> 00:35:31.840
Because someone had to man the door, right?

883
00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:32.120
Yeah.

884
00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:34.260
Like, if everybody leaves, someone has to be there to hand out the candy.

885
00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:35.340
And BJ was like, I'll do it.

886
00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:38.900
I'll never forget one of the years we went, Isabel was Tinkerbell.

887
00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:43.000
And she was so excited when she rang the door that people gave her candy.

888
00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:43.860
She couldn't believe it.

889
00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:45.600
But she was like, what is happening?

890
00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:48.020
But it was really sweet that we all did that.

891
00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:54.260
Even though we'd spent all day working together, we still shared these occasions with one another.

892
00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:55.940
It was really sweet.

893
00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:02.680
I had this, like, professor in college.

894
00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:03.960
I'm going to get really nerdy for a second.

895
00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:06.240
And he was my creative writing professor.

896
00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:10.260
And he used to say, you know, what's a pleasure to do is a pleasure to experience.

897
00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:15.300
So, if you're writing and you love what you're writing, I'm going to love reading it.

898
00:36:15.520 --> 00:36:18.040
And I felt that way about The Office.

899
00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:22.280
We so loved what we were doing that I think it comes through.

900
00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:24.480
I think we cared a lot for each other.

901
00:36:24.700 --> 00:36:27.320
We were very invested in each other's lives.

902
00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:32.860
And I think now the show still resonates because when you watch that, you feel it.

903
00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:34.540
We were genuinely connected.

904
00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:35.220
Yeah.

905
00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:36.280
And we still are.

906
00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:37.160
We still are.

907
00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:39.480
You and I had a text this morning.

908
00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:41.920
Brian Baumgartner texted me this morning.

909
00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:43.940
Rain will then text me constantly.

910
00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:44.600
I know.

911
00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:48.760
Rain, you know, what's great is Rain will text you out of nowhere and just be like, hey, monkey.

912
00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:50.480
And I'm like, hey.

913
00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:52.320
He'll just text you out of nowhere.

914
00:36:52.460 --> 00:36:52.960
I love you.

915
00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:53.620
It's so true.

916
00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:59.940
Like, I feel like if Rain listened to this, he would probably text us and be like, I am not surprised the two of you have just, you can just talk about anything.

917
00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:00.960
Yammering on and on.

918
00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:01.660
On and on.

919
00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:19.830
So because of how we were on the office and the office was my first job, it created an expectation in me that my future job experiences would be similar.

920
00:37:19.830 --> 00:37:25.550
I thought that if I got a job and I went to the producers and I said,

921
00:37:25.850 --> 00:37:37.430
I want this time off to go to my kid's open house or I need to be finished early because this is like my dad's 70th birthday or whatever it is, that they would be like, oh, yes,

922
00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:37.830
of course.

923
00:37:37.830 --> 00:37:40.870
We can manipulate the schedule to make that time for you.

924
00:37:40.970 --> 00:37:42.090
But guess what?

925
00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:43.330
As it turns out.

926
00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:43.650
Yeah.

927
00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:44.430
It's not like that.

928
00:37:44.610 --> 00:37:45.470
It does not work that way.

929
00:37:45.470 --> 00:37:54.630
We had a very special thing on the office and the way that this hit me the year that I was working on my new show, splitting up together, and it was the first season.

930
00:37:54.810 --> 00:38:02.330
And when you do the first season of show, people are especially hyper about everything because every week they might pull the plug.

931
00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:11.250
And I had asked them at the beginning of the season, I said, I just need to be finished by five o'clock on Halloween so that I can go trick or treating with my little kids.

932
00:38:11.870 --> 00:38:13.910
So Halloween was on a Tuesday that year.

933
00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:21.450
The Friday before, the producers came to me and said, Jenna, we've run late this week and we had to drop a couple of scenes.

934
00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:29.310
And the only day that we can rent this crane in order to get this shot that we missed this week is on Halloween.

935
00:38:29.530 --> 00:38:31.910
And we're going to need to tack it on to the end of the day.

936
00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:32.950
So I'm very sorry.

937
00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.810
We'll still get you out by 730, 8 o'clock.

938
00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:38.170
First of all, you have to understand, Jenna's children are young.

939
00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:40.410
They're not trick or treating at 8 o'clock.

940
00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:43.970
No, I said, I said, guys, Halloween's over by 8 o'clock.

941
00:38:44.150 --> 00:38:44.690
It's over.

942
00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:45.990
You know, we're sleeping at 8.

943
00:38:46.130 --> 00:38:46.370
Yeah.

944
00:38:46.570 --> 00:38:47.570
They're in the early window.

945
00:38:47.610 --> 00:38:47.990
Yeah, they're.

946
00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:50.230
It's still a little light out.

947
00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:50.530
Yeah.

948
00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:53.050
It's still a little daylight out at that age.

949
00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:56.030
And then on Sunday morning, you call me up.

950
00:38:56.210 --> 00:38:57.390
Now I'm going to start crying.

951
00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:02.510
You called me up and you said, what are you doing tonight at 5 o'clock?

952
00:39:02.790 --> 00:39:04.450
And I said, nothing.

953
00:39:04.490 --> 00:39:06.230
And you said, I know what you're doing.

954
00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:07.210
You're coming.

955
00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:08.170
Dress up your kids.

956
00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:09.590
Come over to my house.

957
00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:14.090
Stopped to six of my neighbors and they're going to hand out candy to your kids.

958
00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:17.190
I'll get my kids dressed up and we're going trick or treating.

959
00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:19.570
You will not miss Halloween on my watch.

960
00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:20.750
You will not miss it.

961
00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:22.270
I started sobbing.

962
00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:23.810
Sobbing now.

963
00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:24.870
We're both tearing up now.

964
00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:25.610
We're both tearing up.

965
00:39:25.850 --> 00:39:27.550
And we did just that.

966
00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:30.250
I got to dress up my kids in their Halloween costumes.

967
00:39:30.750 --> 00:39:34.230
You dress up your kids in not their Halloween costumes, but in costumes.

968
00:39:34.610 --> 00:39:35.090
Right.

969
00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:36.030
Because you didn't want to.

970
00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:42.650
Our kids still have two huge dress up bins because they love to play dress up.

971
00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:43.390
They still do.

972
00:39:43.390 --> 00:39:46.070
And I told the kids what was going to happen.

973
00:39:46.090 --> 00:39:51.570
And my kids were like, Weston and Harper cannot miss Halloween with their mom.

974
00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:52.690
They cannot miss Halloween.

975
00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:54.430
I said, I know, guys.

976
00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:56.230
So we're going to make Halloween happen for them.

977
00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:57.910
And they were so excited.

978
00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:02.110
And their one thing was, well, we don't want to mess up our actual costumes.

979
00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:05.190
Because they still had their.

980
00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:07.510
They wanted to wait for Halloween to put on their costumes.

981
00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:09.990
And they had their parade at school coming up and all that.

982
00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:10.190
Yeah.

983
00:40:10.330 --> 00:40:14.670
And I said, why don't we all pick out a costume from the dress up bin and we'll all dress

984
00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:15.650
up to trick or treat.

985
00:40:15.970 --> 00:40:17.410
And they were so excited.

986
00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:18.610
And they all dressed up.

987
00:40:18.850 --> 00:40:22.410
Now, here's a question that I have for you, which I've always wondered.

988
00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:28.090
Did your neighbors have candy or did you go to their houses and give them candy to

989
00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:28.430
give me?

990
00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:28.870
Okay.

991
00:40:28.930 --> 00:40:30.690
So I live in a very cute neighborhood.

992
00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:32.450
It's like white picket fences.

993
00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:33.550
It's very cute.

994
00:40:33.750 --> 00:40:34.810
You know all your neighbors.

995
00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:35.290
I know all my neighbors.

996
00:40:35.290 --> 00:40:37.230
You guys do like block parties and stuff.

997
00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:37.350
We do.

998
00:40:37.510 --> 00:40:37.870
We do like.

999
00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:39.450
Like summertime, 4th of July.

1000
00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:40.270
Oh, we do.

1001
00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:40.870
Barbecues.

1002
00:40:41.330 --> 00:40:41.530
Yeah.

1003
00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:42.450
We do barbecues.

1004
00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:46.230
And we have a, oh gosh, what's it called?

1005
00:40:46.490 --> 00:40:47.110
Bocce ball.

1006
00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:48.150
Bocce ball tournament.

1007
00:40:48.850 --> 00:40:51.210
And we just always are hanging out.

1008
00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:54.030
So I knew who I could go to on my street.

1009
00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:55.650
And they all have children.

1010
00:40:56.130 --> 00:41:00.370
And they all know what this means to go trick or treating with your kids when they're

1011
00:41:00.370 --> 00:41:00.610
little.

1012
00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:02.770
I went to them and I said, here's the deal.

1013
00:41:03.250 --> 00:41:07.130
My best friend is going to have to work Halloween and she's not going to be able to be with

1014
00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:09.730
her kids on Halloween and they're little and she won't be able to take them trick or

1015
00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:09.990
treating.

1016
00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:12.450
And I'm going to have them come over here.

1017
00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:13.750
And it would just mean so much to me.

1018
00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:15.150
I said, I will bring you candy.

1019
00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:18.470
